WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.598
Tuvalu's history runs from Polynesian voyaging to independence in 1978.

00:00:06.698 --> 00:00:10.474
Polynesian voyagers, with ties to Samoa and Tonga,

00:00:10.474 --> 00:00:14.477
settled these atolls at least two thousand years ago.

00:00:14.477 --> 00:00:18.933
In 1819 an American captain named Funafuti Ellice's Island.

00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:24.296
The 1860s brought Peruvian slave raiders and Samoan missionary pastors.

00:00:24.296 --> 00:00:27.317
Britain declared a protectorate in 1892.

00:00:28.417 --> 00:00:33.542
The islands became part of the Gilbert and Ellice Islands Colony in 1916.

00:00:33.542 --> 00:00:39.019
In 1942 and 1943 United States forces built an airfield right across Fogafale,

00:00:39.019 --> 00:00:42.951
digging and filling land on a scale still visible today.

00:00:42.951 --> 00:00:47.936
After a 1974 referendum the Ellice Islands separated from the Gilberts,

00:00:47.936 --> 00:00:52.078
and Tuvalu became independent on the first of October 1978.

00:00:53.178 --> 00:00:55.887
The wartime airstrip still dominates Fogafale,

00:00:55.887 --> 00:00:58.948
and the pits dug to build it still hold water today.
